  "activity": {
    "id": "vsepr_theory_exploration",
    "name": "VSEPR Theory and Molecular Geometry",
    "description": "An interactive AR lab where students explore VSEPR theory by analyzing Lewis structures, counting electron domains, predicting electron-domain geometries, and comparing them to observed molecular shapes. Students manipulate interactive 3D models, observe how lone pairs affect molecular geometry, and apply VSEPR principles to real molecules including methane, water, ammonia, phosphorus pentafluoride, sulfur hexafluoride, and xenon tetrafluoride.",
    "language": "en",
    "workplace": "vsepr_chemistry_lab",
    "start": "intro_overview",
    "actions": [
      {
        "id": "intro_overview",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "hololens_student",
        "location": "student_workstation",
        "predicate": "all_five_geometries_video",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Welcome to VSEPR Theory Lab",
          "description": "In this lab, you will explore how electron pairs arrange themselves around a central atom to minimize repulsion. This principle, called VSEPR theory, explains the three-dimensional geometry of molecules. We'll start with an overview of the five fundamental electron-domain geometries: linear, trigonal planar, tetrahedral, trigonal bipyramidal, and octahedral. Then you'll learn how lone pairs modify these geometries to produce the complete set of molecular shapes. Let's begin!"
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "student_workstation",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "all_five_geometries_video"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "intro_overview",
            "type": "action",
            "viewport": "actions",
            "operator": "equal"
          },
          {
            "mode": "voice",
            "id": "intro_overview",
            "viewport": "actions",
            "value": "next",
            "operator": "equal"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "steric_number_concept",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "hololens_student",
        "location": "student_workstation",
        "predicate": "steric_number_explanation",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Step 1: Determining the Steric Number",
          "description": "The first step is to count electron domains around the central atom. An electron domain is any region of high electron density\u2014this includes bonding pairs (in single, double, or triple bonds) and lone pairs. The steric number equals the total number of electron domains. For example, in methane (CH\u2084), the central carbon atom has 4 bonding pairs and 0 lone pairs, giving a steric number of 4. In water (H\u2082O), the central oxygen has 2 bonding pairs and 2 lone pairs, also giving a steric number of 4. Let's examine these Lewis structures."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "lewis_structure_reference_board",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "lewis_structure_methane",
              "poi": "lewis_display_area"
            },
            {
              "target": "student_workstation",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "steric_number_explanation"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "student_workstation",
              "type": "predicate"
            }
          ],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "steric_number_concept",
            "type": "action",
            "viewport": "actions",
            "operator": "equal"
          },
          {
            "mode": "voice",
            "id": "steric_number_concept",
            "viewport": "actions",
            "value": "continue",
            "operator": "equal"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "electron_domain_geometry",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "hololens_student",
        "location": "student_workstation",
        "predicate": "electron_domain_animation",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Step 2: Electron-Domain Geometry from Steric Number",
          "description": "Once you know the steric number, you can predict the electron-domain geometry\u2014the arrangement of all electron domains (bonding and lone pairs) in space. A steric number of 2 gives linear geometry. Steric number 3 gives trigonal planar. Steric number 4 gives tetrahedral. Steric number 5 gives trigonal bipyramidal. Steric number 6 gives octahedral. Each arrangement minimizes electron-pair repulsion. Watch as the electron domains arrange themselves around the central atom. You'll see how repulsion forces the domains apart until they reach an equilibrium configuration that maximizes the distance between them."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "molecular_model_kit",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "electron_domain_animation",
              "poi": "electron_domain_display"
            },
            {
              "target": "molecular_model_kit",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "geometry_prediction_label",
              "poi": "central_atom_position"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "molecular_model_kit",
              "type": "predicate"
            }
          ],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "electron_domain_geometry",
            "type": "action",
            "viewport": "actions",
            "operator": "equal"
          },
          {
            "mode": "voice",
