        "id": "final_summary",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "hololens_student",
        "location": "student_workstation",
        "predicate": "success_confirmation_audio",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Lab Complete: VSEPR Theory Mastered",
          "description": "Excellent work! You have successfully explored VSEPR theory and learned to predict molecular geometry. To summarize: (1) Count the steric number by adding bonding and lone pairs around the central atom. (2) Use the steric number to predict electron-domain geometry (linear, trigonal planar, tetrahedral, trigonal bipyramidal, or octahedral). (3) Distinguish between electron-domain geometry (all domains) and molecular geometry (atoms only). (4) Remember that lone pairs are bulkier than bonding pairs, affecting bond angles. (5) Apply VSEPR to predict real molecular structures. The principle that electron pairs minimize repulsion explains the 3D structure of nearly every molecule. You're now ready to apply these concepts to predict geometries of molecules you'll encounter in your studies."
        },
        "enter": {
